  "meta": {
    "title": "Squid Fall Overture: The First Hour of a Chitin Rush",
    "date": "2026-11-03",
    "lesson": "A jumbo squid carcass drops into the mesopelagic twilight of the Gulf of California. Within minutes copiotrophic bacteria clinging to drifting beak and mantle fragments switch on chitinase to crack the exoskeleton-like beak material, racing phages and grazers that arrive on the scent plume. This scenario isolates the OPENING minutes of succession, before the bloom peaks: chitin-rich shards dominate the particle field, dissolved nutrient is still low below the thermocline, and the founder population must commit hard to carbohydrate digestion (its one universal enzyme) while using chemotaxis to find the sinking fragments before protists and phages converge on the same plume. The take-home: a falling carcass is not food until something can digest its structural polymer, and whoever gets there first and divides fastest captures a resource pulse that will not last.",
    "citation": "Lindsay et al. 2011/2015 Gulf of California squid-fall observations; Pfannkuche et al. baseline chitin-fall decomposition studies",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Gulf of California jumbo squid (Dosidicus gigas) carcass fall, mesopelagic chitin pulse and early Vibrio/Pseudoalteromonas colonization"
  },
